WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:04.871
WIL WILLIS: Jeff,
Craig, congratulations.

00:00:04.904 --> 00:00:07.741
You both made it through to
the "Forged in Fire" final,

00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:12.311
and you're both one step
closer to a check for $10,000.

00:00:12.344 --> 00:00:14.280
In the first two
rounds of competition,

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:16.683
you forged a weapon in
your signature style.

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:19.518
Now we're sending you
back to your home forges,

00:00:19.585 --> 00:00:24.524
where you will forge an
iconic weapon from history--

00:00:24.591 --> 00:00:26.325
the war hammer.

00:00:26.392 --> 00:00:27.527
 Nice.

00:00:27.593 --> 00:00:29.862
WIL WILLIS (VOICEOVER): The
war hammer is a medieval weapon

00:00:29.928 --> 00:00:31.931
developed in the 14th century.

00:00:31.964 --> 00:00:34.133
They came about
primarily as a response

00:00:34.199 --> 00:00:35.902
to advanced armor technology.

00:00:35.968 --> 00:00:39.639
By this time, the armorers had
created suits of plate armor

00:00:39.706 --> 00:00:43.442
that was so hard and smooth that
swords would bounce off them.

00:00:43.509 --> 00:00:46.279
The war hammer could deliver
its full force to the target

00:00:46.346 --> 00:00:50.383
and do serious damage without
having to penetrate the armor.

00:00:50.416 --> 00:00:52.786
Its steel head with
a spike on one side

00:00:52.852 --> 00:00:54.520
had a handle that
varied in length,

00:00:54.587 --> 00:00:57.022
but was generally
just long enough to be

00:00:57.089 --> 00:00:58.524
wielded with one hand.

00:00:58.557 --> 00:01:00.827
The spiked end could
be used for hooking

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:03.797
the target's legs,
reins, or shield,

00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:06.132
or used as an
armor-piercing device.

00:01:06.199 --> 00:01:08.167
As the use of body
armor declined,

00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:10.202
so did the need
for the war hammer.

00:01:10.269 --> 00:01:13.439
However, it can still be seen
today in the popular film

00:01:13.505 --> 00:01:16.242
trilogy "The Hobbit"
and has lent its name

00:01:16.308 --> 00:01:18.945
to the board game "Warhammer."

00:01:19.012 --> 00:01:20.679
WIL WILLIS: Good
luck, bladesmiths.

00:01:20.746 --> 00:01:22.082
We'll see you in five days.

00:01:24.951 --> 00:01:27.454
CRAIG (VOICEOVER): I'm happy
to be here at the home forge.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:28.287
I'm in my element.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:29.822
I know what all my tools are.

00:01:29.889 --> 00:01:31.324
I know how to use my tools.

00:01:31.391 --> 00:01:35.695
So being here at home is
a big advantage for me.

00:01:35.728 --> 00:01:38.063
 Takes a lot of steel to
make the amount of Damascus

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:40.366
that I'm going to make.

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:43.336 line:0%
CRAIG: The plan for today
is to get my Damascus made.

00:01:43.403 --> 00:01:44.871 line:0%
I'm going to take the
two dissimilar metals,

00:01:44.937 --> 00:01:47.807 line:0%
I'm going to heat them up in
my forge, get everything hot.

00:01:47.874 --> 00:01:51.044
I'm going to flux them,
keep the oxygen out of there

00:01:51.077 --> 00:01:52.177
so scales doesn't form.

00:01:52.211 --> 00:01:53.847
Then I'm going to
beat them together.

00:01:58.284 --> 00:02:02.221
I feel a little nervous right
now making this Damascus.

00:02:02.288 --> 00:02:03.623
I can get a bad weld.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:06.192
If I get a bad weld in it,
everything I've done so far

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:07.393
is-- is ruined.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:09.428
It's not a serviceable
weapon at that point.

00:02:09.495 --> 00:02:11.331
I'd have to scrap
it and start again.

00:02:13.699 --> 00:02:15.601 line:0%
JEFF (VOICEOVER): I'm
halfway through day two,

00:02:15.635 --> 00:02:19.139
and I'm getting the horns
on the ram's head now.

00:02:23.676 --> 00:02:26.812
I'm bending the horns
down to have, like,

00:02:26.879 --> 00:02:30.016
a very organic curve to it.

00:02:30.083 --> 00:02:36.589
 Oh, [bleep] [bleep]

00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:40.026
JEFF: I've run out of oxygen
on my oxyacetylene torch.

00:02:40.093 --> 00:02:43.196
I'm not exactly where I
want to be on the horns,

00:02:43.263 --> 00:02:44.731
the hammer head.

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:47.200
 It's starting to wear on me.

00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:48.867
JEFF: I'm just going to have
to come in in the morning,

00:02:48.934 --> 00:02:52.838
focus, and hustle, and make
sure I'm doing things right.

00:02:52.905 --> 00:02:54.073
Don't make any mistakes.

00:02:54.139 --> 00:02:56.209
I don't have any room for that.

00:02:56.276 --> 00:02:58.678 line:7%
[music playing]

00:02:59.679 --> 00:03:01.614 line:0%
 All right, I'm ready
to rock and roll.

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:03.215
CRAIG: Yesterday was
a busy day for me.

00:03:03.282 --> 00:03:05.351
I got the Damascus
forged for my head,

00:03:05.418 --> 00:03:08.688
and I've got it squared to
about the right dimensions.

00:03:08.755 --> 00:03:10.856
Hammer head's going to start
taking shape for me today.

00:03:10.923 --> 00:03:13.860 line:0%
The langets are pretty much
going to lock everything in.

00:03:16.595 --> 00:03:18.231 line:7%
 [laughs]

00:03:18.298 --> 00:03:19.566 line:7%
That is cool.

00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:24.370
CRAIG: I'm looking at these
sharp corners in these slots,

00:03:24.437 --> 00:03:26.271
and I know that I'm going
to have a problem with this

00:03:26.338 --> 00:03:27.740
if I try to full quench it.

00:03:27.807 --> 00:03:32.945
Where I put the langet pockets
in there, that can crack.

00:03:33.012 --> 00:03:34.647
It's thinner than
everything else.

00:03:34.714 --> 00:03:38.351
I could literally end up
with two pieces of hammer.

00:03:38.418 --> 00:03:40.786
I'm going to do a
differential heat treat on it.

00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:43.355
I'm going to try to harden
the front and harden the back

00:03:43.422 --> 00:03:45.425
and leave the middle soft.

00:03:45.458 --> 00:03:47.426
If I crack the hammer
when I quench it,

00:03:47.459 --> 00:03:48.895
that could be disastrous for me.

00:03:53.465 --> 00:03:55.301 line:0%
JEFF: Yesterday,
I was able to get

00:03:55.367 --> 00:03:58.671
the hammer head finished
with all the detail,

00:03:58.738 --> 00:04:00.006
get it quenched.

00:04:00.039 --> 00:04:03.509
And today, I'm getting
into the handle.

00:04:05.678 --> 00:04:08.714
My plan is to add
some upholstery

00:04:08.747 --> 00:04:13.419
rivets down the sides in the
front and back of the handle.

00:04:13.486 --> 00:04:16.789
It's going to be a mix
of unique decorative work

00:04:16.856 --> 00:04:20.426
and traditional-style
war hammer with the spike

00:04:20.493 --> 00:04:21.594
and the hammer head.

00:04:24.363 --> 00:04:26.866
 Pretty happy with that.

00:04:26.899 --> 00:04:29.468
DAVID BAKER: Gentlemen, the
war hammer was primarily

00:04:29.535 --> 00:04:31.337
a close quarters
weapon, but it was

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:34.107
devastating to anything
it went up against,

00:04:34.173 --> 00:04:36.743
which occasionally
was a castle door.

00:04:36.809 --> 00:04:39.712
So I'm going to take several
strikes against this oak door

00:04:39.779 --> 00:04:44.017
to see how well the pick
on your weapon penetrates.

00:04:44.083 --> 00:04:45.684
Craig, you ready?

00:04:45.751 --> 00:04:47.687
 I'm ready.

00:04:47.754 --> 00:04:50.290
[music playing]

00:05:02.768 --> 00:05:06.405
 Well, Craig, this really
felt great in my hands.

00:05:06.471 --> 00:05:07.773
It's got a nice balance to it.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:11.077
Your pick, I don't see
any real distortion on it.

00:05:11.143 --> 00:05:14.413
The shape of your pick, with
that nice drop hook to the end

00:05:14.479 --> 00:05:16.415
of it, really penetrated well.

00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:19.018
And you could see when
I got it in that door

00:05:19.085 --> 00:05:20.619
how hard it was to remove.

00:05:20.686 --> 00:05:21.721
 Yeah.

00:05:21.788 --> 00:05:22.387
 Nicely done.

00:05:22.454 --> 00:05:23.288
CRAIG: Appreciate it.

00:05:23.355 --> 00:05:24.222
 Beautifully executed.

00:05:24.289 --> 00:05:25.357
 Thank you.

00:05:25.424 --> 00:05:26.825
 So, Jeff, your turn.

00:05:26.892 --> 00:05:27.393
You ready?

00:05:27.427 --> 00:05:28.861
 Let's smash it.

00:05:28.894 --> 00:05:29.895
DAVID BAKER: All right.

00:05:29.962 --> 00:05:32.532
[music playing]

00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:50.816
So we started losing pieces.

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:53.052
I can tell that they're on
there only decoratively,

00:05:53.118 --> 00:05:54.954
so I was able to continue on.

00:05:54.987 --> 00:05:58.023
Good thing, head's
still right and tight.

00:05:58.090 --> 00:05:59.225
 All right.

00:05:59.292 --> 00:06:02.328
 The way your pick leans
out towards the front

00:06:02.395 --> 00:06:03.729
gave a lot of penetration.

00:06:03.796 --> 00:06:06.232
I was really able to just
blow parts out of this door.

00:06:06.298 --> 00:06:07.699
 Mm-hm.
DAVID BAKER: Nicely designed.

00:06:07.766 --> 00:06:09.067
 Thank you.
DAVID BAKER: Well executed.

00:06:09.134 --> 00:06:10.603
 Thanks.

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:13.106
 Bladesmiths, now it's
time for the kill test.

00:06:13.172 --> 00:06:15.107
War hammers have
been known to deliver

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:19.979
piercing blows into armor and
crush the person behind it.

00:06:20.046 --> 00:06:22.481
I will deliver strikes
at this ballistic dummy

00:06:22.548 --> 00:06:23.850
that's wearing armor.

00:06:23.916 --> 00:06:26.652
Then we can see how much
lethal damage your war hammer

00:06:26.686 --> 00:06:27.453
can give.

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:28.720
Craig, you're up.
Are you ready?

00:06:28.787 --> 00:06:29.287
 I'm ready.

00:06:29.354 --> 00:06:30.956
Let's do it.

00:06:31.023 --> 00:06:33.593
[music playing]

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:48.340
 Craig, love the
balance of this blade.

00:06:48.374 --> 00:06:49.842
No indentations.

00:06:49.909 --> 00:06:53.946
Now let's see how much
damage it put on the dummy.

00:06:54.013 --> 00:06:59.117
No visible broken bones, but it
definitely did pierce the skin.

00:06:59.184 --> 00:07:01.420
 Nice.

00:07:01.487 --> 00:07:03.923
 This will definitely kill.

00:07:03.989 --> 00:07:05.023
Nicely done, sir.

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:06.058
 Thank you.

00:07:06.125 --> 00:07:06.992
 Jeff, you're up next.

00:07:07.059 --> 00:07:07.760
Are you ready?

00:07:07.827 --> 00:07:08.961
 Yes, sir.

00:07:08.995 --> 00:07:11.531
[music playing]

00:07:22.675 --> 00:07:25.878
 Well, Jeff, looks like the
other langet did break off.

00:07:25.944 --> 00:07:27.146
But you know what?

00:07:27.213 --> 00:07:29.047
This is still very sturdy.

00:07:29.081 --> 00:07:33.686
But let's see how much
damage is done internally.

00:07:33.753 --> 00:07:36.655
He's got some broken ribs right
here, broken bones throughout.

00:07:36.722 --> 00:07:38.291
It went all the way through.

00:07:38.357 --> 00:07:41.293
And even through armor,
you pierced the skin

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:43.595
and you caused some
internal damage.

00:07:43.662 --> 00:07:46.098
Jeff, your weapon will kill.

00:07:46.165 --> 00:07:46.965
 Awesome.

00:07:47.032 --> 00:07:47.867
DOUG MARCAIDA: Good job.

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:48.934
 Thank you.

00:07:48.968 --> 00:07:51.237
DAVID BAKER: Gentlemen,
this is the strength test.

00:07:51.303 --> 00:07:54.439
The war hammer was a favorite
on the medieval battlefield.

00:07:54.506 --> 00:07:55.841
So I'm going to take
your war hammer,

00:07:55.907 --> 00:07:57.643
and to test the
strength and durability,

00:07:57.710 --> 00:08:00.546
I'm going to take several
blows against our stone wall

00:08:00.613 --> 00:08:02.515
and see how much
of this I can move.

00:08:02.581 --> 00:08:03.415
Craig, you're up.

00:08:03.482 --> 00:08:06.018
 All right, let's do it.

00:08:06.085 --> 00:08:08.621
[music playing]

00:08:17.562 --> 00:08:20.233
 Wow, that-- that
held up really well.

00:08:20.266 --> 00:08:22.735
And that's pretty much a
hole I could crawl through.

00:08:22.802 --> 00:08:23.435
[laughter]

00:08:23.502 --> 00:08:24.336
That'll work.

00:08:24.403 --> 00:08:25.771
CRAIG: Perfect.

00:08:25.838 --> 00:08:29.408
 So the handle, what I do like
is I could swing it one-handed.

00:08:29.475 --> 00:08:31.176
But with two hands,
I could generate

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:33.145
a heck of a lot of energy.

00:08:33.212 --> 00:08:33.913
Beautiful job.

00:08:33.946 --> 00:08:34.446
 Excellent.

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:35.280
Excellent.

00:08:35.347 --> 00:08:36.215
Thank you.

00:08:36.281 --> 00:08:38.283
 Jeff, you're up.

00:08:38.350 --> 00:08:40.085
 Let's knock it down.

00:08:40.152 --> 00:08:42.755
[music playing]

00:08:49.495 --> 00:08:50.829
 It's long.

00:08:50.896 --> 00:08:52.498
And when I was swinging, it
was way out in front of me

00:08:52.564 --> 00:08:55.334
as opposed to being
close and in control.

00:08:55.367 --> 00:08:57.302
It shot some sparks off.

00:08:57.369 --> 00:08:58.070
JEFF: Right.

00:08:58.103 --> 00:08:59.271
I seen that.

00:08:59.338 --> 00:09:01.106
 All in all, a
good performance.

00:09:01.173 --> 00:09:01.941
 Great.

00:09:04.509 --> 00:09:07.546
WIL WILLIS: Craig, Jeff,
you were given five days

00:09:07.613 --> 00:09:10.583
at your home forge to
fabricate war hammers,

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:14.287
and they both performed very
well in our weapons tests.

00:09:14.353 --> 00:09:16.956
But there can only be one
"Forged in Fire" champion.

00:09:20.692 --> 00:09:23.529
Craig, you are the
"Forged in Fire" champion.

00:09:23.596 --> 00:09:24.462
Congratulations.

00:09:24.529 --> 00:09:25.631
 Thank you.

00:09:25.664 --> 00:09:29.168
 Jeff, your weapon
did not make the cut.

00:09:29.235 --> 00:09:31.903
Please surrender
your war hammer.

00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:34.006
JEFF: I respected
the judges' decision.

00:09:34.072 --> 00:09:38.610
If I knew what I knew now, I
would have changed some things.

00:09:38.677 --> 00:09:40.012
 Craig, congratulations.

00:09:40.079 --> 00:09:42.280
You are the "Forged
in Fire" champion.

00:09:42.347 --> 00:09:43.916
 It was a fun challenge.

00:09:43.982 --> 00:09:49.088
 Craig, you will also be
receiving a check for $10,000.

00:09:49.154 --> 00:09:50.256
 I'm just stoked.

00:09:50.289 --> 00:09:51.457
It's crazy.

00:09:51.524 --> 00:09:53.859
And this experience, it
was a grueling process.

00:09:53.925 --> 00:09:55.894
You know, I got to do
something that I really enjoy

00:09:55.961 --> 00:09:57.429
and that I'm really
passionate about.

00:09:57.496 --> 00:09:58.730
I came here for my son.

00:09:58.797 --> 00:10:00.900
I mean, it doesn't get
any better than that.

00:10:00.967 --> 00:10:03.736
[music playing]

